Droidloom on the erhai Ubuntu distribution
==========================================

Three packages make up Droidloom: droidloom-runtime (host programs),
droidloom-image (Android vendor image and framework overrides) and
droidloom-base (AOSP system, system_ext and product images). Runtime and
image always come from the same upstream release; the image names the base
release it needs.

droidloom-image and droidloom-base are the upstream Arch Linux ARM packages
repacked unchanged. droidloom-runtime holds upstream's files too, except its
programs (/usr/bin/droidloom* and /usr/lib/droidloom/droidloom-package-helper):
they are built for Ubuntu 26.04 arm64 from

    https://github.com/EvilBorsch/droidloom
    commit @HOST_COMMIT@

which is upstream's release @PKGVER@-@PKGREL@, commit
@COMMIT@, with these changes:

@HOST_CHANGES@

Two of them change what a person sees. An application launched while
Droidloom is stopped (a launcher icon, or droidloomctl launch) starts
Droidloom as droidloomctl start does, waits for Android and then opens; this
does not enable droidloom.service for the next session. The launcher entries
of Android's own applications (packages com.android.*) are named with
"(Android)" after Android's label, "Settings (Android)", so they are not
taken for the host's Settings and Files.

One file is this packaging's own, not upstream's:
/usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-droidloom-loop.rules. Android's system images and
APEX packages are mounted through loop devices, about fifty of them, and
udisks offered each to file managers as a volume ("959 MB Volume"). The rule
marks the loop devices whose file is Droidloom's or Android's with
UDISKS_IGNORE=1; other loop devices are not touched. Installing the package
makes a running udev read the rule and apply it to the loop devices there
are.

The build recipe is docs/EB-BUILD.md in that repository (branch eb/main).

The kernel needs Binder with binderfs, EROFS with LZ4, policy routing and the
other options listed in the research report (research/droidloom/README.md in
the distribution workspace). `droidloom-doctor --format json --pretty` checks
most of them without changing anything.

First start, as the desktop user inside the Wayland session (not with sudo):

    droidloomctl start

It asks for administrator authentication once (pkexec) to write
/etc/droidloom/cell.json, /etc/droidloom/runtime.env, the polkit rule
/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/49-droidloom.rules and Android's data directory
/var/lib/droidloom/users/UID/data. Then:

    droidloomctl applications
    droidloomctl launch com.android.settings
    droidloomctl install /path/to/app.apk
    droidloomctl stop

A launch starts a stopped Droidloom by itself; that takes as long as
droidloomctl start. To start Android with every graphical session:
    systemctl --user enable droidloom.service

Removing the packages keeps /etc/droidloom/cell.json and Android's data;
purging droidloom-runtime removes /etc/droidloom. Android's data in
/var/lib/droidloom is only removed by hand.
